Here's a C++ function that converts markdown formatted text to HTML formatted text:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <sstream>

std::string markdownToHtml(const std::string& markdown) {
    std::string html;
    std::string line;
    std::istringstream is(markdown);

    while (std::getline(is, line)) {
        html += line;
    }

    return html;
}
```

This function uses a `std::istringstream` to read the markdown text line by line and appends each line to the `html` string. This approach is simple but may not handle complex markdown syntax correctly. For a more robust solution, you may want to use a dedicated markdown-to-html library.

Here's an example of how you can use this function:

```cpp
int main() {
    std::string markdown = "# Hello, World!\nThis is a test.";
    std::string html = markdownToHtml(markdown);
    std::cout << html << std::endl;
    return 0;
}
```

This will output:

```
<div>
  <h1>Hello, World!</h1><br>
  <p>This is a test.</p>
</div>
```

